NNAAMMEE
     ppss — display process status

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     ppss [[--]AAaacceeffHHhhjjkkLLllmmrrSSTTuuvvwwxx] [--MM _c_o_r_e] [--NN _s_y_s_t_e_m] [--OO _f_m_t] [--oo _f_m_t]
        [--pp _p_i_d] [--tt _t_t_y] [--UU _u_s_e_r] [--WW _s_w_a_p]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ppss utility displays information about active processes.  When given
     no options, ppss prints information about processes of the current user
     that have a controlling terminal.

     The information displayed is selected based on a set of keywords (and for
     even more control, see the --LL, --OO, and --oo options).  The default output
     format includes, for each process, the process's ID, controlling termi‐
     nal, state, CPU time (including both user and system time), and associ‐
     ated command.

     The options are as follows:

     --AA      Display information about processes for all users, including
             those without controlling terminals.

     --aa      Display information about processes for all users with control‐
             ling terminals.

     --cc      Do not display full command with arguments, but only the exe‐
             cutable name.  This may be somewhat confusing; for example, all
             sh(1) scripts will show as “sh”.

     --ee      Display the environment as well.

     --ff      Arrange processes into a tree.  Each command is ordered and pre‐
             fixed with indentation text showing sibling and parent/child re‐
             lationships.  If either of the --mm and --rr options are also used,
             they control how sibling processes are sorted relative to each
             other.

     --HH      Also display information about kernel visible threads.

     --hh      Repeat the information header as often as necessary to guarantee
             one header per page of information.

     --jj      Print information associated with the following keywords: user,
             pid, ppid, pgid, sess, jobc, state, tt, time, and command.

     --kk      Also display information about kernel threads.

     --LL      List the set of available keywords.  This option should not be
             specified with other options.

     --ll      Display information associated with the following keywords: uid,
             pid, ppid, cpu, pri, nice, vsz, rss, wchan, state, tt, time, and
             command.

     --MM _c_o_r_e
             Extract values associated with the name list from the specified
             core instead of the running kernel.

     --mm      Sort by memory usage, instead of by start time ID.

     --NN _s_y_s_t_e_m
             Extract the name list from the specified system instead of the
             running kernel.

     --OO _f_m_t  Add the information associated with the space or comma separated
             list of keywords specified, after the process ID, in the default
             information display.  Keywords may be appended with an equals
             sign (‘=’) and a string.  This causes the printed header to use
             the specified string instead of the standard header.

     --oo _f_m_t  Display information associated with the space or comma separated
             list of keywords specified.  Keywords may be appended with an
             equals sign (‘=’) and a string.  This causes the printed header
             to use the specified string instead of the standard header.

     --pp _p_i_d  Display information associated with the specified process ID.

     --rr      Sort by current CPU usage, instead of by start time ID.

     --SS      Change the way the process time is calculated by summing all ex‐
             ited children to their parent process.

     --TT      Display information about processes attached to the device asso‐
             ciated with the standard input.

     --tt _t_t_y  Display information about processes attached to the specified
             terminal device.

     --UU _u_s_e_r
             Only display processes belonging to username or UID _u_s_e_r.

     --uu      Display information associated with the following keywords: user,
             pid, %cpu, %mem, vsz, rss, tt, state, start, time, and command.
             The --uu option implies the --rr option.

     --vv      Display information associated with the following keywords: pid,
             state, time, sl, re, pagein, vsz, rss, lim, tsiz, %cpu, %mem, and
             command.  The --vv option implies the --mm option.

     --WW _s_w_a_p
             When not using the running kernel, extract swap information from
             the specified file.

     --ww      Use 132 columns to display information, instead of the default,
             which is the window size.  If the --ww option is specified more
             than once, ppss will use as many columns as necessary without re‐
             gard for window size.

     --xx      Display information about processes without controlling termi‐
             nals.

