SSTTAANNDDAARRDDSS
     The ppss utility is compliant with the IEEE Std 1003.1-2008 (“POSIX.1”)
     specification, except that the flag [--GG] is unsupported and the flags
     [--ppttUU] support only single arguments, not lists.

     The flags [--ddeeffggllnnuu] are marked by IEEE Std 1003.1-2008 (“POSIX.1”) as
     being an X/Open System Interfaces option.  Of these, [--ddffggnn] are not sup‐
     ported by this implementation of ppss; behaviour for the flags [--eelluu] dif‐
     fers between this implementation and the X/Open System Interfaces option
     of IEEE Std 1003.1-2008 (“POSIX.1”).

     The flags [--ccffHHhhjjkkLLMMmmNNOOrrSSTTvvWWwwxx] are extensions to IEEE Std 1003.1-2008
     (“POSIX.1”).

     Only the following keywords are recognised by IEEE Std 1003.1-2008
     (“POSIX.1”): aarrggss, ccoommmm, eettiimmee, ggrroouupp, nniiccee, ppccppuu, ppggiidd, ppiidd, ppppiidd,
     rrggrroouupp, rruusseerr, ttiimmee, ttttyy, uusseerr, and vvsszz.

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     A ppss command first appeared in Version 3 AT&T UNIX.

CCAAVVEEAATTSS
     When printing using the ccoommmmaanndd keyword, a process that has exited and
     has a parent that has not yet waited for the process (in other words, a
     zombie) is listed as “⟨defunct⟩”, and a process which is blocked while
     trying to exit is listed as “⟨exiting⟩”.  ppss makes an educated guess as
     to the file name and arguments given when the process was created by ex‐
     amining memory or the swap area.  The method is inherently somewhat unre‐
     liable and in any event a process is entitled to destroy this informa‐
     tion, so the names cannot be depended on too much.  The uuccoommmm (account‐
     ing) keyword can, however, be depended on.

     The information displayed is only a snapshot of a constantly changing
     system.

